  "body": {
    "id": "prop:bounded-range-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If exact aggregate support equality is replaced by the standard bounded-range condition \\(Y_a\\in[\\ell_a,u_a]\\), every nonempty branch contributes its full attained interval regardless of \\(\\tau_{a,b}^r(P)\\). If the score restrictions are also removed, the branch polytope is the full box and the resulting arm endpoints are the classical Manski worst-case bounds.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Y_a",
      "a",
      "l_a",
      "u_a",
      "tau_a_b_r",
      "P",
      "F_a_b_r"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:branch-reduction",
      "def:branch-polytope",
      "def:arm-endpoints"
    ],
    "justification": "The reduction verifies that the support filter, rather than bounded outcomes alone, causes branch pruning.",
    "gap": "This is a required sanity reduction, not a novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "Readers can distinguish the exact-support contribution from standard worst-case bounded-outcome identification.",
    "proof_tex": "Replace exact aggregate support equality by only \\(Y_a\\in[\\ell_a,u_a]\\).  The converse construction in \\(\\text{lem:branch-reduction}\\) then realizes every \\(x_a\\in\\mathcal F_{a,b}^r(P)\\), including a singleton box vertex.  Since a nonempty branch polytope is compact and convex, its linear arm-mean image is the full attained interval\n\\[\n [L_{a,b}^r(P),U_{a,b}^r(P)],\n\\]\nwith no condition involving \\(\\tau_{a,b}^r(P)\\).  This proves the first assertion and isolates exact aggregate support, rather than boundedness, as the source of branch pruning.\n\nIf the score restrictions are also removed, the defining inequalities disappear and\n\\[\n \\mathcal F_{a,b}^r(P)=\\prod_{j\\in\\mathcal M_a}[\\ell_a,u_a].\n\\]\nBecause every \\(p_j>0\\), coordinatewise minimization and maximization yield\n\\[\n L_a=c_a+\\ell_a\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal M_a}p_j,qquad\n U_a=c_a+u_a\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal M_a}p_j.                                       \\tag{44}\n\\]\nConsistency fixes the factual contribution \\(c_a\\), while the missing conditional means can independently attain every value in their support interval.  Thus (44) is both an outer bound and attainable, and it is exactly the classical bounded-outcome worst-case completion associated with \\cite{Manski1990}.",
